/-!
# The complete source point-core implication

The original V is fully moved by L₁ and lies in O₂(L₁). It lies in
O₂(G₁) when L₁ is normal there, or when the containing normal component
has the Ξ₇ quotient model. Under the genuine source hypotheses, the
original seven-space and its G₁-normal closure lie in O₂(G₁).

With LocalComponents supplied, failure of V ≤ O₂(G₁) leaves precisely
the L₅(2), M₂₄ and J₄ rows, with the source residual Y contained in K₁.
The literal chief-factor intersections give an irreducible elementary
image of order eight under failure of core containment. The normal
component quotient is identified with its actual star image, and the
action of G₁/O₂(G₁) on U₁/V₁ is constructed on representatives. The original
M₁* retains its SL₂(2) × SL₃(2) core quotient and contains a normal elementary
eight-group under failure. The actual intersection M₁* ∩ K₁* contains a
Sylow subgroup of K₁* of relative index 63. In the linear component row,
this identifies the intersection as a middle parabolic in an actual basis
of the five-space. Its tensor radical excludes the normal eight-group,
proving V ≤ O₂(G₁) in that row without an assumed parabolic comparison.
In the M₂₄ row, the native normal-eight catalogue places the normalizer in
an octad stabilizer or a proper subgroup of a trio stabilizer. Neither can
contain this Sylow overgroup of index 63, so the Mathieu row is also excluded
through the original component quotient. In the J₄ row, the actual action
on U₁/V₁ is faithful on the component: inherited simplicity and an explicit
exterior transvection establish this. The seed involution moves at most
seven dimensions, contradicting the native lower bound of twelve.

Thus all point-core rows are proved conditional on LocalComponents.
The original weak-closure, point-control, generation-one and terminal
consumers now have their point-core premise discharged. Construction of
LocalComponents and the general normalizer containments stay with this
owner; the final exterior assembly stays with qt-d-1251-source.

Source: Aschbacher--Smith, 12.5.8(1), printed p.820.
-/
